---
import PerformanceChart from '../../components/PerformanceChart.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { dxyNote, ethNote, fxNote, type Currency } from '../../lib/currency';
import { dataFor } from '../../lib/data';
import { formatPct } from '../../lib/format';
import { currencyPaths } from '../../lib/routes';
import { rebaseCovering, totalReturnPct } from '../../../pipeline/rebase';
import { PERF_LABELS_CLIENT, perfStartOptions, toAssetSeries } from '../../lib/perf-shared';

export const getStaticPaths = currencyPaths;

const { currency } = Astro.props as { currency: Currency };
const { benchmarksHistory } = dataFor(currency);

const assets = toAssetSeries(benchmarksHistory);
const options = perfStartOptions(assets, benchmarksHistory.dailyDays);
const initial = options.find((o) => o.selected) ?? options[0];
// The tiles describe the same window the build drew, from the same function, so
// a figure here and the height of a line cannot disagree.
const rebased = initial ? rebaseCovering(assets, initial.start) : null;
const ranked = [...(rebased?.series ?? [])].sort(
  (a, b) => totalReturnPct(b) - totalReturnPct(a),
);
const ethSeries = benchmarksHistory.series.find((s) => s.asset === 'eth')?.sourceSeries;
// Hoisted out of the prose: an interpolation at the start of a line loses the
// space before it, and the built page read "the benchmarks2016-08-05".
const firstOf = (asset: string): string =>
  assets.find((a) => a.asset === asset)?.rows[0]?.date ?? '—';
/**
 * The deepest preset, and how far apart its series finish.
 *
 * The note about why log is the default used to assert this from memory — "over
 * a decade BTC's index reaches five figures while the benchmarks stay near 200"
 * — and every clause was refuted by the page under it. The deepest preset here
 * begins in 2017, not a decade ago; BTC ends at 820, not five figures; the
 * benchmarks end between 107 and 546, not near 200; and on the *default* view
 * BTC (153) finishes below gold (230). The argument for a log axis survives all
 * of that, so it is made from the measured spread instead.
 */
const deepestOption = options.reduce<(typeof options)[number] | undefined>(
  (best, o) => (best === undefined || o.start < best.start ? o : best),
  undefined,
);
const deepestSeries = deepestOption ? rebaseCovering(assets, deepestOption.start) : null;
const finals = (deepestSeries?.series ?? []).map((s) => s.finalIndex);
const deepest = {
  label: deepestOption?.label ?? '—',
  start: deepestOption?.start ?? '—',
  spread:
    finals.length < 2
      ? null
      : {
          low: Math.round(Math.min(...finals)),
          high: Math.round(Math.max(...finals)),
          factor: `${(Math.max(...finals) / Math.min(...finals)).toFixed(1)}×`,
        },
};
const btcFrom = firstOf('btc');
const spFrom = firstOf('sp500');
const ethFrom = firstOf('eth');
---

<Base
  currency={currency}
  title="blockplot · performance"
  description="BTC against ether, the S&P 500, gold and the dollar index, every series indexed to 100 at a shared start date."
>
  <h1 class="visually-hidden">Performance</h1>

  <dl class="stat-grid">
    {
      ranked.map((s) => (
        <div class="stat">
          <dt>{PERF_LABELS_CLIENT[s.asset] ?? s.asset}</dt>
          <dd class={`num ${totalReturnPct(s) < 0 ? 'down' : 'up'}`}>{formatPct(totalReturnPct(s))}</dd>
          <dd class="sub num">index {s.finalIndex.toFixed(0)}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <section class="chart-section">
    {/* No range or scale in the heading. It named "5y · log scale" from the
        build's defaults and never changed, so after pressing max and linear it
        described neither the chart nor the caption below it. The chart's own
        controls carry that state, and the caption carries the base. */}
    <h2>Indexed to 100 · BTC against its benchmarks</h2>
    <PerformanceChart currency={currency} />
    <p class="method-note">
      Every series is divided by its own close on the shared base date and multiplied by 100, so
      the lines are directly comparable whatever their units — an index, an ounce price, a dollar
      index and two coins. The base is the first day on which <em>every</em> drawn series has a
      price, not the day the button names: BTC and ether trade every day where the S&amp;P 500 and
      gold do not, so a Saturday start would otherwise index BTC two days before the S&amp;P and
      bake that difference into every later point. The caption under the chart states the base
      date it actually used.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      This file starts at {btcFrom} for BTC — the first weekly point, a few days after the
      underlying daily history begins — at {spFrom} for the benchmarks, since FRED publishes the
      S&amp;P 500 as a rolling decade, and at {ethFrom} for ether. "max" is therefore the
      earliest start at which all five exist, not the earliest date in the file: a chart carrying
      one line for seven years and then five is not the comparison this page is for. Every preset
      consequently begins after every series does, so none of them drops a line — the machinery to
      exclude a late-starting series and name it exists for a future range, not for something you
      will meet here.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      Two consequences of the weekly resolution, stated rather than smoothed over. In the weekly
      section the crypto legs land on Sundays and the market legs on Fridays — a seven-day week's
      last close against a five-day week's — so no single day holds all five, and the caption names
      the <em>week</em> they share instead of asserting a date three of them never traded. And a
      deep preset's base can only move a week at a time, so a figure can step when the rolling
      window crosses a week boundary and the base lands on the next Monday: BTC's 5y return moves
      about 30 points across one such rollover, because that is what BTC did in that week of 2021.
      The presets are snapped to week boundaries so the base is at least stable between rollovers
      rather than sliding a day at a time. The right-hand edge is not uniform either — sources
      publish on different schedules, so the newest close is a day older for some series than
      others.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      Log is the default, which is a claim about the data rather than a preference. On the deepest
      preset ({deepest.label}, from {deepest.start}) the series end
      {' '}{deepest.spread === null ? 'at a wide spread' : (
        <>
          between {deepest.spread.low} and {deepest.spread.high} — a factor of
          {' '}{deepest.spread.factor}
        </>
      )}, and a linear axis gives that whole range to the strongest line while the rest share
      what is left. Log makes equal vertical distances equal <em>ratios</em>, so every series keeps
      its shape. Linear is the honest view for a short window, where ratios and differences agree.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      Resolution: the last {benchmarksHistory.dailyDays} days of every series are daily, and
      everything older is one point per ISO week — its last close, never an average, since an
      average is a number no market printed. A decade of five daily series is 92 KB gzipped and all
      of it has to be embedded, because the start date is chosen in the browser and this site makes
      no request to fetch more; this rule is a third of that for a difference invisible at any
      screen width. Total returns are price returns: no dividends on the S&amp;P, no yield, no
      fees, no taxes.{fxNote(currency)}{ethNote(currency, Boolean(ethSeries))}{dxyNote(currency)}
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
